 ---
 name: floorplan
-description: Draw a floorplan of an app's codebase (one repo or several side by side), an interactive node-graph map where every subsystem, module, file and HTTP route has a human-readable name and a one-line description, connected by the imports and HTTP calls between them, at several zoom levels. It is saved as a self-contained floorplan/index.html in the codebase. Use whenever the user wants to visualize, map, diagram, document or get an architectural overview of a codebase, repo or monorepo, understand how their app works or is connected under the hood, onboard onto unfamiliar code, see how the frontend talks to the backend, or update an existing floorplan, even if they don't say "visualize". Also use it before any code exists: when the user shares a PRD, a spec or a description of something they want to build and asks what pieces it needs or how they'd fit together, it draws a blueprint, the same map planned from the document.
+description: Draw a floorplan of an app's codebase (one repo or several side by side), an interactive node-graph map where every subsystem, module, file and HTTP route has a human-readable name and a one-line description, connected by the imports and HTTP calls between them, at several zoom levels. It is saved as a self-contained floorplan/index.html in the codebase. Use whenever the user wants to visualize, map, diagram, document or get an architectural overview of a codebase, repo or monorepo, understand how their app works or is connected under the hood, onboard onto unfamiliar code, see how the frontend talks to the backend, or update an existing floorplan, even if they don't say "visualize". When a floorplan/ map already exists, also use it before exploring the code for a task (which files and lines to read, who calls a function, how a flow works, what a change touches): it saves most of the tokens spent searching. Also use it before any code exists: when the user shares a PRD, a spec or a description of something they want to build and asks what pieces it needs or how they'd fit together, it draws a blueprint, the same map planned from the document.
 argument-hint: "[path-to-codebase-root] [--refresh]"
 allowed-tools:
   - Bash(node ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/build.mjs *)
@@ -128,6 +128,17 @@ The codebase root is the argument if one was given, otherwise the current direct
 
    Then **report.** Give the URL, then describe the architecture you mapped in 3–5 lines, in the user's language, including anything surprising: cycles, god-files, dead areas, coupled subsystems. Always mention the **high-severity Health items** (`graph.health`; the build output and graph.json list them). Hard-coded passwords/keys and endpoints anyone can change data through are things the owner needs to hear about. Never repeat a secret's value. Say that `architecture.json` and `descriptions/` are theirs to edit, and that after more work they can just ask to "update the floorplan", which re-reads only what changed. Give them those plain words rather than a command: in Claude Code the plugin's command is `/floorplan:floorplan` (`/floorplan` as a personal skill), and in Codex it's `$floorplan`. On a first run, mention in one line that the map stays out of git. In Claude Code, when the build said `CLAUDE.LOCAL.MD  not added`, end by asking once whether future Claude Code sessions in this project should use the map: you'd add a short note to their personal, uncommitted CLAUDE.local.md. Add it (`build.mjs <root> --claude-md`) only on a yes, and don't ask again on later updates if they said no. On an update, start with what changed for what they asked (it's highlighted under **Recent work**).
 
+## Working on the code: use the map instead of exploring
+
+Coding agents spend most of their tokens finding code (on SWE-bench, reads are roughly three quarters of a run). When a map exists and you (or a subagent) are about to work on a task in this codebase, look it up first instead of globbing, grepping and reading whole files:
+
+- `node floorplan/query.mjs context "<task in a few words>"`: the files and exact line ranges to read, ranked from the map (names, descriptions, functions, endpoints) and spread through imports, requests and calls; with callers, endpoints, screens and tests. Read those ranges; widen only if they are not enough.
+- `node floorplan/query.mjs fn <name>`: where a function is, what calls it, what it calls (instead of grepping for callers before changing it).
+- `node floorplan/query.mjs map`: the codebase in about 1,500 tokens, for a fresh session or a subagent's brief.
+- trace.mjs (how a flow works end to end) and impact.mjs (what a change touches) below.
+
+When you hand work to subagents, give each the `context` output for its piece instead of asking it to explore. The map can lag: confirm line numbers in the code before editing, and refresh the map after large changes.
+
 ## How does something work, step by step? (trace)
 
 When the user asks how something works end to end ("what happens when someone pays?", "walk me through the booking screen", "which functions does POST /orders reach?"), trace it instead of reading files:
